A Boundary-Characterisation Programme for the Engel–Dreizler Excess-Charge Theory:

Structural Obstruction to the Removal of the Nuclear-Charge Restriction

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Benguria and Siedentop (arXiv:2611.14497, 2026; hereafter BS26) have established the uniform molecular excess-charge bound

Q:=N-Z_{\mathrm{tot}}<\frac{5913}{1000}\,\lambda^{3/2}K \tag{0.1}

for the exchange-free Engel–Dreizler functional, under the invariant coupling restrictions

\gamma:=\alpha\lambda\le\frac{1}{1233},\qquad \chi_k:=\alpha^3Z_k^2\le\frac{1}{137}\quad(1\le k\le K). \tag{0.2}

The proof architecture is built on three pillars: exterior Sommerfeld estimates depending only on \gamma; a convex nonlinear comparison in P=F(p) whose high-density failure is represented by a non-negative Newton potential S; and a quartic coercivity estimate in which nuclear repulsion together with a signed localised Hardy inequality controls the source mass \int S. The exterior layer is already charge- and geometry-independent; the restrictions (0.2) enter exclusively through the high-density source-control and coercivity steps.

This thesis programme asks whether the boundary encoded in (0.2) can be moved, and what the movement—or its impossibility—reveals about the Engel–Dreizler functional itself. The central analytical vehicle is a high-density scaling analysis of the radial atomic Euler–Poisson system near the nucleus. The analysis identifies an ultra-relativistic concentration regime in which the Fermi momentum scales as p(0)\sim Z^{3/2}/\sqrt{\log Z} and the core mass as N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim Z^{3/2}/(\log Z)^{3/2}, so that the integrated source mass grows with Z rather than saturating. This structural growth is inconsistent with the absorption mechanism of BS26 at fixed \gamma as \chi\to\infty. The programme therefore targets a boundary-characterisation theorem: a rigorous proof that the removal of the \chi_k restriction is impossible within the BS26 mechanism, together with a classification of the precise inequality at which the mechanism fails, and a systematic investigation of whether any alternative coercivity device can evade the obstruction. A rigorous obstruction is treated as a co-equal primary outcome with a rigorous extension. The programme is atomic-first, source-mass-centric, and audit-driven, and is structured so that the Stage 1 rational-arithmetic audit and the Stage 2 scaling diagnostics are independently publishable contributions.

---

1. The Established Theory and Its Architectural Boundaries

1.1 What BS26 proves

BS26 prove that under (0.2), every finite-mass electronic minimiser of the exchange-free Engel–Dreizler functional whose total energy (including nuclear repulsion) is non-positive satisfies (0.1). The bound is independent of \sum_k Z_k and of nuclear positions; no minimum nuclear separation is assumed. At \alpha=1/137, \lambda=1/9, the numerical value is Q<0.219\,K. The energy hypothesis is automatic for atoms and for attained electronic minimisers at local minima of the Born–Oppenheimer energy. A positive-energy extension is quantified by

Q<\frac{5913}{1000}\lambda^{3/2}K+\frac{\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}E_+,\qquad \varepsilon=48\,e^{-180/(1233\alpha\lambda)}, \tag{1.1}

so that at the boundary of the domain, \varepsilon\sim e^{-180}.

1.2 The three architectural pillars

(A) Exterior Sommerfeld estimates. The comparison uses the harmonic function H(x)=\sum_k|x-R_k|^{-1} and depends only on \gamma. BS26 state explicitly that the proof uses “no symmetry of the density, restriction on the total nuclear charge, or minimum nuclear separation”. The exterior layer is therefore already uniform in the variables that the \chi_k restriction constrains.

(B) The convex comparison and its high-density defect. In the transformed variable P=F(p), the comparison differential inequality fails on the high-density set \{p>p_{\mathrm{cut}}\}. The failure is represented by a non-negative Newton potential S, and BS26 prove the source-mass bound

\int_{\mathbb{R}^3}S\le36\pi\alpha\,N_{\mathrm{high}}. \tag{1.2}

The exponentially large cutoff is p_{\mathrm{cut}}=\exp(180/(1233\gamma)), and the absorption step at the end of the proof requires this cutoff to make N_{\mathrm{high}} small enough.

(C) The coercivity split. In the molecular case, the negative correction of a signed localised Hardy inequality is controlled by nuclear repulsion. In the atomic case, a different global Hardy device is used. BS26 note that the molecular proof “combines molecular Sommerfeld estimates with a convex nonlinear comparison” and that a “quartic coercivity estimate, obtained from nuclear repulsion and a signed localized Hardy inequality, controls the total mass of this source”. The atomic case does not have nuclear repulsion at its disposal and must therefore rely on the global device. This architectural split is the structural reason why any new mechanism must first be tested atomically.

1.3 The precise locus of the restrictions

The restriction \gamma\le1/1233 enters the proof through the exterior comparison and through the numerical smallness required for the absorption. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 enters exclusively through the coercivity step: it is the condition under which the quartic form remains positive after the local Hardy correction is subtracted, given the nuclear-repulsion control available in the molecular setting. In the atomic setting, the global Hardy device replaces nuclear repulsion, but the constant 1/137 is still inherited from the same quartic estimate.

The audit of Stage 1 is designed to classify each occurrence of these constants as tight (saturable by the proof’s own inequalities), loose (improvable by better rational constants), or structural (forced by the mechanism). The numerical scaling analysis of Section 2 provides the evidence that at least the \chi-restriction is structural in a precise sense: the source mass that must be absorbed by the coercivity step grows with Z at fixed \gamma, so no improvement of the constants in the coercivity estimate can compensate.

