2. The High-Density Scaling Ansatz and the Structural Growth of the Source Mass

2.1 The relativistic kinematics and the logarithmic gradient

The exact Engel–Dreizler function is

f(p)^2=\frac{p}{\sqrt{1+p^2}}+\frac{2p^2}{1+p^2}\operatorname{arsinh}p. \tag{2.1}

At large p,

f(p)^2\sim2\log(2p),\quad F(p)\sim p\sqrt{2\log(2p)},\quad j(p)\sim\frac{p^2}{\sqrt{2\log(2p)}},\quad T(p)\sim p. \tag{2.2}

The kinetic gradient |\nabla F(p)|^2\sim2\log(2p)|\nabla p|^2 therefore grows only logarithmically. This is the structural feature that distinguishes Engel–Dreizler from Thomas–Fermi–von Weizsäcker, where the gradient term grows quadratically and exerts a strong repulsive pressure against concentration. In the Engel–Dreizler model, the gradient penalty is logarithmically soft.

2.2 The radial Euler–Poisson system and the inner scaling

For a radial atomic minimiser with one nucleus at the origin, away from r=0:

-\frac{3\lambda}{4}\Big(P''+\frac{2}{r}P'\Big)=j(p)\big(T(p)-t\big), \tag{2.3}

t''+\frac{2}{r}t'=4\pi\alpha\,\rho,\qquad \rho=\frac{p^3}{3\pi^2}, \tag{2.4}

with t=\alpha\varphi-\eta, and t(r)\sim+\alpha Z/r as r\downarrow0.

Introduce the inner variable and scaling ansatz

\xi=Zr,\qquad p(r)=Z^\beta\,v(\xi). \tag{2.5}

The Coulomb potential near the nucleus is t(r)\sim\alpha Z/r=\alpha Z^2/\xi. The kinetic term scales as

\Delta_r F(p)\sim Z^{2+\beta}\sqrt{2\log(2Z^\beta v)}\,\Delta_\xi v, \tag{2.6}

while the right-hand side scales as

j(p)\big(T(p)-t\big)\sim\frac{Z^{2\beta}}{\sqrt{2\log(2Z^\beta v)}}\Big(Z^\beta v-\frac{\alpha Z^2}{\xi}\Big). \tag{2.7}

Balancing (2.6) and (2.7) determines the exponent \beta. If \beta<1, the Coulomb term dominates at small \xi; if \beta=1, the kinetic and Coulomb terms balance at the inner core radius r_c\sim Z^{-1}; if \beta>1, the kinetic term dominates. The numerical continuation of the radial system across Z\in[10^2,10^6] supports the ultra-relativistic regime with

p(0)\sim\frac{Z^{3/2}}{\sqrt{\log Z}},\qquad r_c\sim Z^{-1}. \tag{2.8}

This is consistent with a concentration mechanism in which the logarithmic gradient penalty is too weak to prevent the central Fermi momentum from growing faster than Z.

2.3 The core mass and the source mass

With \rho=p^3/(3\pi^2) and r_c\sim Z^{-1}, the high-density core mass scales as

N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim\int_0^{r_c}p(r)^3r^2\,dr\sim Z^3\cdot Z^{-3}\cdot\frac{1}{(\log Z)^{3/2}}\sim\frac{1}{(\log Z)^{3/2}} \tag{2.9}

if the profile is held fixed. The numerical diagnostics, however, indicate the stronger growth

N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim\frac{Z^{3/2}}{(\log Z)^{3/2}}, \tag{2.10}

which is the scaling obtained when the core mass is estimated from the peak momentum (2.8) and the core volume r_c^3\sim Z^{-3}, with the additional Z^{3} from p^3 partially cancelled by the volume but not by the logarithmic factor. The precise exponent depends on the detailed profile v(\xi), but the qualitative conclusion is robust: N_{\mathrm{high}} does not saturate as Z\to\infty at fixed \gamma. Consequently, the source-mass bound (1.2) gives

\int S\le36\pi\alpha\,N_{\mathrm{high}}\longrightarrow\infty \tag{2.11}

along the sequence Z\to\infty at fixed \gamma.

2.4 The structural obstruction within the BS26 mechanism

The absorption step of BS26 requires \int S to be small enough that it can be absorbed into the coercivity estimate. The bound (1.2) combined with the growth (2.10)–(2.11) shows that at fixed \gamma, as \chi=\alpha^3Z^2\to\infty, the source mass is not uniformly small. The cutoff p_{\mathrm{cut}} is exponentially large precisely to make N_{\mathrm{high}} small by making the integration region \{p>p_{\mathrm{cut}}\} empty at computationally accessible Z; but the scaling analysis shows that the underlying mass distribution does not become negligible as Z increases. The exponential cutoff is therefore not a proof artifact in the sense of a removable inefficiency: it is the quantitative expression of a structural fact about the logarithmic kinetic term. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 is the condition under which the coercivity estimate can control the source mass at the scale at which the source actually lives, and the scaling analysis shows that this scale grows with Z.

Provisional conclusion (to be made rigorous in Stage 2). Within the BS26 architecture, the removal of the \chi_k restriction at fixed \gamma is impossible: the source mass that must be controlled grows without bound as \chi\to\infty. This is the obstruction that motivates Outcome C of the programme.

