3. The Boundary-Characterisation Programme

3.1 Research questions

RQ1 (Audit). At which precise points do \gamma\le1/1233 and \chi_k\le1/137 enter? Which are tight, loose, or structural?

RQ2 (Scaling). What is the rigorous inner-core scaling of p(r) for the Engel–Dreizler functional? Does the logarithmic gradient force N_{\mathrm{high}} to grow as Z\to\infty at fixed \gamma, and with what exponent?

RQ3 (Source mass). Can \int S be bounded independently of p_{\mathrm{cut}} by a device that does not pass through the BS26 absorption mechanism?

RQ4 (Molecular uniformity). If an atomic obstruction is established, can a conditional extension survive molecular assembly uniformly in nuclear separations, including the coalescence limit?

A negative answer to RQ2 or RQ3 is a rigorous obstruction; a negative answer to RQ4 is a sharp statement about the limits of geometry uniformity. All are legitimate outcomes.

3.2 Stage 1: Independent audit (months 1–6)

The audit reconstructs the BS26 proof in exact rational arithmetic, isolating every occurrence of \gamma\le1/1233 and \chi_k\le1/137 and classifying each as tight, loose, or structural. The deliverable is a written entry-point map, accompanied by a rational-arithmetic notebook recording the slack at each finite inequality. The audit is the foundation for every subsequent stage: it determines whether a modest improvement of the constants is available (a publishable extension in its own right), and it identifies the precise inequality at which the structural obstruction of Section 2.4 is felt.

3.3 Stage 2: Atomic scaling and the obstruction (months 6–16)

The central task is to convert the heuristic scaling analysis of Section 2 into a rigorous statement. The dichotomy for the inner profile v(\xi) must be established: either v(\xi) remains bounded uniformly as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, or it diverges in a quantifiable way. The numerical evidence points to divergence, but the rigorous proof requires a careful analysis of the inner Euler–Poisson system with the logarithmic nonlinearity.

Three analytical routes are pursued in parallel, with the understanding that any one of them may supply the obstruction:

1. Direct scaling inequality. Prove that the energy of the inner core is bounded below by a term proportional to N_{\mathrm{high}}^{1+2/3}/(\log N_{\mathrm{high}})^{1/2}, which would force N_{\mathrm{high}} to grow when the Coulomb attraction scales as Z^{2} while the kinetic penalty scales only logarithmically.
2. Comparison with a model problem. Replace f(p)^2 by 2\log(2p) in the high-density region and solve the resulting model Euler–Poisson system exactly. If the model exhibits N_{\mathrm{high}}\to\infty, and if the Engel–Dreizler solution can be shown to dominate the model solution in the inner region, the obstruction transfers.
3. Coercivity-gap analysis. Exhibit a sequence of trial configurations (not necessarily minimisers) on which the quartic coercivity form of BS26 becomes negative as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, thereby showing that the coercivity estimate cannot hold beyond a finite \chi regardless of the constants. This is the most direct route to a structural obstruction, because it targets the precise inequality at which the restriction enters.

The numerical diagnostics—p(0), r_c, and the diagnostic core masses at moderate thresholds—support the analytical work by supplying the scaling exponents and by testing candidate trial configurations. They are reported as numerical evidence, never as asymptotic theorems.

The Stage 2 deliverable is either (a) a rigorous atomic extension on a domain strictly larger than (0.2), or (b) a rigorous obstruction showing that Q must grow with \chi at fixed \gamma within any mechanism that controls the source mass through a bound of the form (1.2). The decision point at month 12 determines which branch the programme pursues.

3.4 Stage 3: Conditional molecular extension and geometry uniformity (months 16–28)

If Stage 2 yields an obstruction, the molecular stage shifts from attempting an unconditional extension to characterising the largest domain on which a conditional extension survives. Two directions are pursued.

Direction 1: Screened local Hardy. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 controls the bare nuclear charge seen by the local Hardy inequality. After screening by the electron cloud, the effective charge in the inner region may be smaller than the bare Z_k, allowing the local Hardy inequality to hold for a larger bare Z_k provided the electron density is sufficiently high. The Chen–Wu architecture for two-centre Thomas–Fermi–Dirac–von Weizsäcker—comparison with the neutral Thomas–Fermi density, screened-potential localisation, exterior L^1 estimates—supplies a template, though no estimate transfers verbatim because the Engel–Dreizler kinetic term is \operatorname{arsinh}-type. The task is to quantify the screening correction and to determine whether it shifts the critical \chi by a bounded factor or by an unbounded one.

Direction 2: Union-type local mass bound. Prove that the high-density set is contained in a union of balls centred at the nuclei, with radii controlled by Z_k and \gamma and independent of the other nuclei, and with mass in each ball bounded uniformly. The coalescence limit R_k\to R_l is the critical test: when two nuclei approach, the harmonic function H develops a stronger singularity and the local Hardy correction changes character. A union bound that survives coalescence and reduces to the Stage 2 atomic bound in the isolation limit R_k\to\infty is the target.

The Stage 3 deliverable is a molecular theorem on the largest domain compatible with the Stage 2 obstruction, with geometry uniformity proved by a union-type local mass bound supplemented by screening, or a rigorous demonstration that geometry uniformity fails in the coalescence limit.

3.5 Stage 4: Synthesis (months 28–36)

The synthesis combines Stages 1–3 into a boundary-characterisation theorem with the following structure:

· a precise statement of the obstruction: the inequality at which the BS26 mechanism fails as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, together with the scaling law that quantifies the failure;
· a precise statement of any conditional extension: the largest domain on which a uniform excess-charge bound holds, and the additional hypotheses required;
· sharpness examples: trial configurations or asymptotic profiles that saturate the obstruction;
· a comparison with the BS26 constants, identifying which of 1/1233 and 1/137 are tight and which are loose;
· a discussion of what a genuinely different coercivity mechanism would need to accomplish in order to evade the obstruction.

