4. Categorisation Protocol

Every claim is tagged as one of:

· Proved: established in BS26 or in the present work, with a complete proof.
· Conditional: established only under explicitly stated hypotheses not known to hold in general.
· Conjectured: proposed without proof, with motivation.
· Numerical: supported by computation with reported precision, mesh convergence, and residuals.

No numerical observation is presented as an asymptotic theorem. No failure of a specific mechanism is presented as a disproof of the desired bound unless a rigorous obstruction is supplied.

---

5. Expected Outcomes and Their Status

Outcome C (primary, obstruction). A rigorous proof that within the BS26 architecture the source mass cannot be uniformly controlled as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, together with an identification of the precise inequality at which the mechanism fails and the scaling law that quantifies the growth. This is the outcome for which the numerical evidence of Section 2 provides the strongest support. It is a structural theorem about the Engel–Dreizler functional: the logarithmic kinetic term is too weak to prevent the high-density core mass from growing, and the exponential cutoff is the quantitative expression of this weakness.

Outcome B (conditional extension). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma,\chi)K holds on a domain strictly larger than (0.2), with an explicit trade-off between \gamma and \chi, combined with a sharpness example showing that the trade-off cannot be improved within the current mechanism. This outcome is compatible with Outcome C: the obstruction constrains the path along which \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, while a conditional extension describes the largest domain reachable by optimising constants and screening.

Outcome A (unconditional removal of \chi_k). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma)K for all \chi_k>0 at fixed \gamma. The scaling analysis of Section 2 makes this outcome unlikely within the BS26 mechanism, but the programme does not exclude it a priori: an alternative coercivity device that exploits the logarithmic gradient in a fundamentally different way might evade the obstruction. Stage 2 is designed to test this possibility before the programme commits to Outcome C.

Outcome D (supporting, always deliverable). The Stage 1 rational-arithmetic audit, the corrected radial solver, and the diagnostic scaling laws for p(0), r_c, and the core masses. These are publishable independently as reproducibility and benchmarking contributions.

---

6. Feasibility and Risk Management

The hardest analytic infrastructure is already in place. BS26 supply the exterior comparison, the nonlinear comparison, the source-mass representation, the coercivity architecture, and the absorption. The programme inherits these and asks only where the restrictions can be relaxed, or why they cannot.

The audit is bounded: reconstruct a published proof, identify the entry points of two parameters, and re-verify rational inequalities. This is directly supported by BS26’s own reduction of finite inequalities to rational arithmetic.

The scaling analysis is well-posed: the radial Euler–Poisson system is a boundary-value problem amenable to Chebyshev collocation with logarithmic grading, and the diagnostic quantities are directly computable. The numerical continuation across Z\in[10^2,10^6] already provides the scaling exponents that motivate the obstruction.

The atomic-first strategy is forced by the proof structure. BS26 uses nuclear repulsion in the molecular coercivity step and a different global Hardy device in the atomic step; any new mechanism must therefore be tested atomically first.

Decision point at month 12. If the scaling analysis has not produced a rigorous obstruction, the programme pivots to Outcome B (conditional extension) and allocates the remaining Stage 2 time to optimising the constants and the screening argument. If a rigorous obstruction is achieved, Stage 3 becomes a study of conditional molecular extensions and the limits of geometry uniformity. In either case, the Stage 1 audit and the numerical diagnostics are secured by month 12 and constitute a defensible thesis core.

---

7. Summary

The BS26 theory has settled the uniform excess-charge theorem inside the domain (0.2). The present programme addresses the question that BS26 leaves open: whether the boundary of that domain is movable. The high-density scaling analysis points to a structural obstruction: the logarithmic kinetic gradient of the Engel–Dreizler functional is too weak to prevent the inner core mass from growing as Z^{3/2}/(\log Z)^{3/2}, so the source mass that the coercivity step must absorb grows with Z at fixed \gamma. The programme is therefore organised around a boundary-characterisation theorem in which the obstruction is a primary result and any extension is conditional on evading it. The programme is atomic-first, source-mass-centric, and audit-driven, and it is structured so that a rigorous negative result is as valuable as a rigorous positive one.

