Appendix A. Supplementary Literature Commentary

A.1 Precise statement and architecture of Benguria–Siedentop (arXiv:2611.14497). The BS26 theorem asserts that under

\alpha\lambda\le\frac{1}{1233},\qquad \alpha^3Z_k^2\le\frac{1}{137}\quad(1\le k\le K),

every finite-mass electronic minimiser whose total energy (including nuclear repulsion) is non-positive satisfies

N-\sum_{k=1}^K Z_k<\frac{5913}{1000}\lambda^{3/2}K.

The paper states explicitly that the proof uses “no symmetry of the density, restriction on the total nuclear charge, or minimum nuclear separation”. The synthesis combines “molecular Sommerfeld estimates with a convex nonlinear comparison in the variable F(p)”; the failure of the latter comparison at very high density “is represented by a nonnegative Newton potential,” and a “quartic coercivity estimate, obtained from nuclear repulsion and a signed localized Hardy inequality, controls the total mass of this source”. All finite numerical inequalities are reduced to rational arithmetic. These statements directly support the audit classification: the \gamma-restriction enters through the exterior estimates and the absorption step, while the \chi_k-restriction enters through the quartic coercivity step.

A.2 Chen–Wu (arXiv:2610.09997) two-centre TFDW bound. Chen and Wu prove that for two distinct nuclei of total charge Z, the number of bound electrons satisfies

N\le Z+C Z^{153/154}(1+\log Z)^{3/8},

with constants independent of the nuclear positions and the nuclear charges. The proof relies on “a comparison with the neutral Thomas–Fermi density and on localization and exterior L^1-estimates coupled through the screened potential”, and the authors state that the results “could be extended to molecules with any fixed number K of nuclei”. It must be emphasised that the exponent 153/154 arises from the quadratic gradient term of TFDW; the Engel–Dreizler gradient grows as |\nabla p|^2\log p, so the Chen–Wu exponent cannot transfer verbatim. The architecture serves as a template, not as a black box.

A.3 Frank–Nam–Van den Bosch (2017) single-centre TFDW theorem. In Thomas–Fermi–Dirac–von Weizsäcker theory, a nucleus of charge Z>0 can bind at most Z+C electrons, where C is a universal constant. The proof is “obtained through a comparison with Thomas–Fermi theory which, as a by-product, gives bounds on the screened nuclear potential and the radius of the minimizer”. This establishes the benchmark for the non-relativistic case: with a quadratic gradient term, the excess charge is O(1). The appearance of the \chi_k-restriction in the Engel–Dreizler setting reflects the fact that the logarithmic gradient provides a weaker concentration penalty and cannot deliver the same uniformity at large Z.

A.4 The \operatorname{arsinh} structure and boundedness in Engel–Dreizler. The literature states that “the renormalization in Engel’s and Dreizler’s derivation leads to the \operatorname{arsinh} function in Weizsäcker’s term, which ensures the lower boundedness of \mathcal{E}_{c,V}^{\mathrm{TFWD}}(\rho) for all nuclear charges Z”. By contrast, in the ultra-relativistic limit (without the \operatorname{arsinh} correction), the Coulomb potential and the kinetic term “have the same scaling behavior, at least for high momenta,” and the non-renormalised model is not expected to be well-defined for arbitrarily large nuclear charge. The \operatorname{arsinh} therefore provides a minimal boundedness mechanism, but whether its logarithmic growth is sufficient to suppress the high-density source mass at fixed \gamma is a separate question. The numerical evidence points to a negative answer.

A.5 Critical exponent in generalised TFW. Benguria and Siedentop identify p=3/2 as the critical exponent in generalised Thomas–Fermi–Weizsäcker functionals with kinetic term p^\alpha: for 3/2<\alpha<2 the excess-charge conjecture holds, while at \alpha=3/2 “the behavior changes from a uniform bound in Z to a linear bound at the critical coupling 4\sqrt{\pi} of the nonlinear term”. The Engel–Dreizler kinetic term grows as p^2/\sqrt{\log p}, which lies between the logarithmic and quadratic regimes, so the generalised TFW critical-exponent theory does not apply directly. The existence of a critical exponent 3/2 nevertheless suggests that in logarithmically corrected models a transition from uniform boundedness to growth may occur at a finite \chi, consistent with the obstruction conclusion of Section 2.4.

