Appendix B. Numerical Supplement and Convergence Record

B.1 Parameters and grid. The computations use Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto collocation with N=600 nodes and a logarithmic mapping

r(x)=r_{\min}\Big(\frac{r_{\max}}{r_{\min}}\Big)^{(x+1)/2},\qquad r_{\min}=10^{-6},\quad r_{\max}=10^{3}.

The physical constants are \alpha=1/137.035999 and \lambda=1/9. For each Z, a Thomas–Fermi-type initial guess starts the Newton–Krylov iteration, with convergence declared when the L^\infty residual falls below 10^{-10}. The continuation in Z proceeds from 10^{2} to 10^{6} with logarithmic step size 2^{1/2}.

B.2 Numerical fits of scaling exponents. The fit for p(0) gives

p(0)\approx C_p\,Z^{3/2}(\log Z)^{-1/2},

and the linear regression of \log p(0) on \log Z-\tfrac12\log\log Z over Z\in[10^{3},10^{6}] yields a slope of 1.498\pm0.006, consistent with the exponent 3/2. The core radius r_c, defined by p(r_c)=1, satisfies r_c\propto Z^{-1.002\pm0.004}. The diagnostic core mass N_{>p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}} at p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}=10^{3} obeys

N_{>10^{3}}\approx C_N\,Z^{3/2}(\log Z)^{-3/2},

with a regression slope of 1.49\pm0.01. These fits cover a finite Z-range and are classified as Numerical under the categorisation protocol; they do not constitute asymptotic theorems.

B.3 Source-mass bound and the BS26 absorption scale. The column \int S in the diagnostic table is computed from the right-hand side of the BS26 source-mass bound 36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}}, with N_{\mathrm{high}} taken as N_{>p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}} at p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}=10^{3}. At Z=10^{6}, the value is \int S\approx 5.0\times10^{2}, whereas the BS26 absorption step requires this quantity to remain O(1) as \gamma approaches its critical value. The order-of-magnitude discrepancy is the numerical expression of the obstruction discussed in Section 2.4.

B.4 Condition number and round-off error. On the logarithmic grid, the condition number of the discrete radial Laplacian grows as N^{4}; at N=600, the two-dimensional spectral condition number is of order 10^{10}–10^{11}. In double precision this limits the effective digits of p(0) to about 5–6 and of r_c to about 4–5. The trapezoidal-rule error in N_{>p_0} is controlled by the C^{1} regularity of the integrand at the core boundary and is estimated as O(N^{-2}). Table B.1 records the relative changes at Z=10^{4} when the grid is doubled from N=600 to N=1200. All changes are smaller than the reported fit uncertainties, so the grid error does not affect the qualitative conclusions of B.2.

