Appendix D. Formal Proof Sketch of the Obstruction

D.1 Inner scaling lemma (draft). Let p be a regular radial solution of the Euler–Poisson system (2.3)–(2.4) with t(r)=\alpha Z/r+O(1) near the origin. Suppose there exist Z_0 and \beta>0 such that p(0)\ge cZ^\beta for all Z\ge Z_0. Then there exist constants C_1,C_2>0, depending on \gamma but not on Z, such that for Z\ge Z_0,

N_{>p_0}(Z)\ge C_1 Z^{3\beta-3}(\log Z)^{-\beta/2}

for all p_0\le p(0)/2.

Proof sketch. By monotonicity of p on [0,r_c] (valid for radial minimisers), p(r)\ge p(0)/2 for r\le r_c:=p^{-1}(p(0)/2). Hence

N_{>p(0)/2}\ge\frac{4\pi}{3\pi^2}\Big(\frac{p(0)}{2}\Big)^3\frac{r_c^3}{3}.

The Coulomb lower bound on t together with the balance in (2.3) gives r_c\ge c'Z^{-1}. Substituting p(0)\ge cZ^\beta yields N_{>p_0}\ge C_1 Z^{3\beta-3}. The logarithmic factor comes from the 1/\sqrt{\log p} correction in j(p) in (2.2); it does not change the power but contributes (\log Z)^{-\beta/2}. \square

If the numerical fit gives \beta=3/2, Lemma D.1 yields N_{\mathrm{high}}\gtrsim Z^{3/2}(\log Z)^{-3/4}, which is weaker than the (\log Z)^{-3/2} in (2.10) but still implies N_{\mathrm{high}}\to\infty. The obstruction requires only this divergence, not the precise exponent.

D.2 Model Euler–Poisson system. In the region \{p>p_*\gg1\}, replace the exact Engel–Dreizler function by f(p)^2\simeq2\log(2p). Introduce u:=p\sqrt{2\log(2p)}, the leading term of F(p). The model system is

-\frac{3\lambda}{4}\Delta u=\frac{p^2}{\sqrt{2\log(2p)}}\big(p-t\big),\qquad \Delta t=4\pi\alpha\frac{p^3}{3\pi^2}. \tag{D.1}

Set \xi=Zr and p(r)=Z^\beta v(\xi). At \beta=1, the leading balance gives

\xi^2v''+2\xi v'=\mu(\xi)\,\frac{v^2}{\sqrt{\log Z}}\Big(v-\frac{\alpha}{\xi}\Big), \tag{D.2}

where \mu(\xi)>0 is slowly varying. The scaling analysis of (D.2) shows that v(\xi) grows at most as \xi^{-1} as \xi\to0 (to match the Coulomb singularity), so p(r)\sim Z/r in the shell r\sim Z^{-1}. The leading core mass is

N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim\int_0^{Z^{-1}}\Big(\frac{Z}{r}\Big)^3r^2\,dr\sim Z^{3}\int_0^{Z^{-1}}r^{-1}\,dr,

which diverges logarithmically at r\to0. The exact Engel–Dreizler solution cuts off this divergence at r\sim Z^{-1} (since p is finite at r=0), but the cutoff radius shrinks as Z grows, and the truncated mass still grows as Z^{3/2}(\log Z)^{-3/2}. This gives a model-level mechanism for (2.10).

D.3 Precise statement of the obstruction proposition (draft). Fix \gamma\in(0,1/1233]. Assume:

1. p_{\mathrm{cut}}(\gamma)=\exp(180/(1233\gamma)) as in BS26;
2. the absorption step requires \int S\le c_0, where c_0=c_0(\gamma) is the maximal source mass allowed by the coercivity estimate;
3. the inner scaling satisfies Lemma D.1 with \beta=3/2.

Then there exists \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}=\chi_{\mathrm{crit}}(\gamma,c_0)<\infty such that for \chi>\chi_{\mathrm{crit}} no minimiser satisfies the BS26 absorption mechanism. Equivalently, at fixed \gamma, the BS26 architecture cannot extend the excess-charge bound (0.1) uniformly to \chi\to\infty.

Argument. By Lemma D.1, \int S\ge c_1 Z^{3/2}(\log Z)^{-3/2}. The BS26 absorption condition \int S\le c_0 requires Z\le Z_*(\gamma,c_0)<\infty. Set \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}=\alpha^3Z_*^2. Since \chi\propto Z^2, \chi>\chi_{\mathrm{crit}} is equivalent to Z>Z_*, at which point the source mass exceeds the absorption capacity. \square

The Conditional status of the proposition stems from assumption 3: the inner scaling currently has numerical support but no rigorous proof. Replacing assumption 3 with a rigorous inner-scaling theorem is the central analytical task of Stage 2.

D.4 Necessary versus sufficient conditions. The obstruction of D.3 is an obstruction within the BS26 absorption mechanism, not an obstruction of the Engel–Dreizler functional itself. It proves that any argument relying on the source-mass bound (1.2) and feeding it into the quartic coercivity estimate cannot handle \chi\to\infty uniformly at fixed \gamma. It does not exclude a fundamentally different coercivity mechanism that avoids the bound \int S\le36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}}. Stage 2, task 3—testing weighted L^4–L^3 interpolation, Brezis–Lieb splitting, and Moser iteration—is designed precisely to determine whether such a mechanism exists. If all candidates fail, the obstruction is upgraded from “mechanism-internal” to “model-level”; if one succeeds, Outcome A remains possible. This logical distinction is the concrete expression of the Proved / Conditional / Conjectured separation in the categorisation protocol.

